{
  "evidence": "TEXT_D: Recruit 27,500 nurses and 4,600 doctors; add 10,000 hospital beds; free personal care; restore free dental and eye checks; move toward scrapping prescription charges; Children's Rights Commissioner; end mixed wards; ban age discrimination; free off-peak bus travel for pensioners/disabled; VED incentives for clean vehicles; carbon tax, emissions trading, ≥10% renewables by 2010, GM crop moratorium, national doorstep recycling to 60%, marine protected areas, flood defence task force; 6,000 additional police and community officers, restorative/youth justice, independent police complaints, stronger victims’ rights; retain minimum nuclear deterrent while pursuing disarmament; increase basic state pension, annual minimum wage review, disabled winter fuel support; abolish university tuition fees and restore grants; 0.7% GNI aid and stronger climate commitments.\nTEXT_E: Recruit 20,000 nurses and 10,000 doctors; faster NHS treatment free at point of use; recruit 10,000 teachers and raise secondary standards; add 6,000 police and reform criminal justice to improve detection, punishment, rehabilitation; retain winter fuel payment for pensioners and raise minimum wage to £4.20; engage in Europe with action on global poverty and climate change; maintain low inflation, low mortgages, and sound public finances.",
  "reasoning": "Because Care and Sanctity are the electorate’s top priorities, TEXT_D aligns better by offering broader, concrete harm-reduction measures (e.g., free personal care, scrapping prescription charges, extensive support for pensioners/disabled) and robust purity/naturalness policies (carbon tax, renewables targets, GM moratorium, large-scale recycling). It still satisfies moderate Authority and Loyalty with added police capacity and retention of a nuclear deterrent, whereas TEXT_E leans more on authority and macroeconomic stability with comparatively fewer sanctity-focused and comprehensive care commitments.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_D"
}
